### What are all seven OOP concepts?

The four pillars (encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism) define how individual classes are built. The three relationship concepts (association, aggregation, composition) define how objects collaborate. Together they form a complete picture of object-oriented design.

### What is association?

Association is the most general relationship between two objects. Object A knows about object B and can call its methods. Neither owns the other. Both have independent lifecycles. In Java, one class holds a reference to another, typically through a field or constructor parameter.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TradeService {
    private final NotificationService notificationService; // association: uses

    public TradeService(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
        // TradeService does not CREATE notificationService: it is injected.
    }

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        // ... execute trade logic ...
        notificationService.sendTradeConfirmation(trade);
    }
}
// TradeService and NotificationService are associated.
// Neither owns the other. Spring injects the dependency.
```

### What is aggregation?

Aggregation is a weak HAS-A relationship. The parent contains children, but children have independent lifecycles. They can exist without the parent. If the parent is destroyed, children survive.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class TradingDepartment {
    private String departmentName;
    private List<Trader> traders = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addTrader(Trader trader) {
        traders.add(trader);
        // Department does NOT create the trader.
    }

    public List<Trader> dissolve() {
        List<Trader> surviving = new ArrayList<>(traders);
        traders.clear();
        return surviving; // traders still exist, can join another department
    }
}
// department = null; traders still exist in memory.
// In JPA: NO cascade delete.
```

### What is composition?

Composition is a strong OWNS-A relationship. The parent owns children. Children cannot exist without the parent. If the parent is destroyed, children are also destroyed. The parent controls the entire lifecycle: creation, modification, and destruction.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Account {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final List<Transaction> transactions = new ArrayList<>(); // composition: owns

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        transactions.add(new Transaction("CREDIT", amount, LocalDateTime.now()));
        // Account CREATES the transaction. It owns it.
    }

    public void close() {
        transactions.clear(); // transactions destroyed with account
        // In JPA: @OneToMany(cascade = ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    }
}
// Transaction cannot exist without an Account.
```

### How do association, aggregation, and composition compare?

| Aspect | Association | Aggregation | Composition |
|--------|------------|-------------|-------------|
| Relationship | Uses-A | Has-A (weak) | Owns-A (strong) |
| Lifecycle | Independent | Independent | Bound to parent |
| Child survives parent? | Yes | Yes | No |
| Who creates child? | External | External | Parent |
| UML notation | Arrow (-->) | Hollow diamond | Filled diamond |
| JPA cascade | None | None | cascade ALL + orphanRemoval |
| Example | Service uses Provider | Branch has Traders | Account owns Transactions |

### Why is OOP important?

OOP provides three core benefits that make large-scale software manageable.

```java
// 1. MODULARITY: each class is a self-contained unit
// AccountService: handles balance, deposits, withdrawals
// TradeService: handles trade execution
// NotificationService: handles SMS/email
// Each can be developed, tested, and deployed independently.

// 2. MAINTAINABILITY: change one class without breaking others
// Change NotificationService from SMS to email: TradeService unchanged.
// Add NRIAccount: InterestBatchService unchanged (polymorphism).

// 3. REAL-WORLD MODELING: objects map to real things
// Account object = real bank account at National Bank of India
// Trade object = real stock trade on NSE
// Customer object = real customer like Rajesh
```

### How do all seven concepts appear in one banking system?

```java
// ENCAPSULATION: Account.balance is private
// ABSTRACTION: PaymentService.transfer() hides NEFT/UPI complexity
// INHERITANCE: SavingsAccount extends Account
// POLYMORPHISM: account.calculateInterest() dispatches to correct override
// ASSOCIATION: SettlementService uses ClearingHouse (injected)
// AGGREGATION: TradingDepartment has Traders (traders survive dissolution)
// COMPOSITION: Trade owns Fees (fees die with the trade)
```

### What are common mistakes when modeling relationships?

The most common mistake is confusing aggregation and composition. Ask: can the child exist meaningfully without the parent? If yes, it is aggregation. If no, it is composition.

```java
// WRONG: treating composition as aggregation
// Loan-EMI is composition, not aggregation.
// An EMI without a Loan is meaningless.
// If you model it as aggregation (no cascade delete),
// closing a loan leaves orphan EMI rows in the database.

// CORRECT:
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "loan", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<EMI> emis; // composition: EMIs die with the loan
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring, association is the default relationship: services use other services via constructor injection.
- Domain-Driven Design maps aggregation and composition to Aggregate Roots: the root entity owns its children.
- Excessive inheritance hierarchies are a code smell. Prefer composition (HAS-A) over inheritance (IS-A) for code reuse.
- Modern Java (records, sealed classes) adds new tools: records enforce immutability (encapsulation), sealed classes control inheritance hierarchies.
